        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "cdc1737f-0580-5b68-8769-ce44149d601e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Second Battle of Guam, First Battle of Guam, Battle of Antietam, Maryland Campaign\n- Places: Guam, Mariana Islands, Agat, Sharpsburg, Maryland, Antietam Creek\n- Organizations: United States, Japan\n- Topics: Pacific campaign, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- The First Battle of Guam took place in 1941, when Japan captured Guam from the United States during the Pacific campaign of World War II.\n- The Second Battle of Guam lasted from 21 July to 10 August 1944 and was the American recapture of the Japanese-held U.S. territory of Guam in the Mariana Islands.\n- The beaches of Agat were one of the American landing sites in the 1944 Battle of Guam.\n- The Battle of Antietam, also called the Battle of Sharpsburg especially in the South, was fought on September 17, 1862, near Sharpsburg, Maryland, and Antietam Creek as part of the Maryland Campaign.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "86ff61d3-d3eb-5cf6-a8c0-56e8f9082024",
        "text": "Page: Battle of Guam (1944). Sentence: The Second Battle of Guam (21 July – 10 August 1944) was the American recapture of the Japanese-held island of Guam, a U.S. territory in the Mariana Islands captured by the Japanese from the U.S. in the 1941 First Battle of Guam during the Pacific campaign of World War II.\nPage: Battle of Antietam. Sentence: The Battle of Antietam , also known as the Battle of Sharpsburg, particularly in the South, was fought on September 17, 1862, near Sharpsburg, Maryland and Antietam Creek as part of the Maryland Campaign.\nPage: Agat Invasion Beach. Sentence: The beaches of Agat were one of the landing sites of American forces in the 1944 Battle of Guam, in which the island was retaken from occupying Japanese forces.",
